\begin{abstract}
We present an autonomous coastal stabilization physics engine governed by the BIA-1 multi-agent scientific intelligence architecture. The system integrates a high-fidelity Eulerian--Lagrangian Navier--Stokes solver with multi-phase sediment transport, Exner bed evolution, and adaptive boundary friction modeling. A four-phase validation protocol is introduced, combining invariant checks, a two-test benchmark suite, automated tolerance gates, and autonomous optimization clearance. The benchmark suite consists of a dam-break sediment surge test and the Beji--Battjes submerged bar experiment, jointly spanning shock-dominated and dispersive wave-transforming regimes. Only after achieving full convergence parity with laboratory data does the system permit autonomous shoreline geometry adaptation. This establishes a rigorous foundation for resilient coastal defense design under extreme hydrodynamic forcing.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Coastal environments are governed by nonlinear, multi-phase fluid dynamics involving wave transformation, sediment transport, morphological evolution, and extreme storm-driven forcing. Traditional numerical models require extensive manual tuning and operator oversight, limiting scalability and reliability under high-energy conditions.

The BIA-1 autonomous multi-agent architecture provides a closed-loop scientific reasoning system capable of ingesting multi-modal telemetry, executing high-performance physics kernels, monitoring invariants, enforcing strict tolerance gates, and autonomously reshaping shoreline geometries. This publication formalizes the governing equations, validation protocol, and autonomous optimization clearance required for trustworthy coastal stabilization design.

\section{Governing Equations for Multi-Phase Coastal Flow}

\subsection{Mixture Continuity}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial \rho_m}{\partial t}
+ \nabla \cdot (\rho_m \mathbf{u}) = 0,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\rho_m = (1 - c)\rho_w + c\rho_s,
\end{equation}
is the mixture density, $c$ is sediment volume fraction, and $\mathbf{u}$ is barycentric velocity.

\subsection{Momentum Conservation}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial (\rho_m \mathbf{u})}{\partial t}
+ \nabla \cdot (\rho_m \mathbf{u}\otimes\mathbf{u})
= -\nabla p
+ \nabla \cdot \bm{\tau}
+ \rho_m \mathbf{g}
+ \mathbf{F}_{\text{drag}}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Suspended Sediment Transport}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial c}{\partial t}
+ \nabla \cdot (c \mathbf{u})
+ \nabla \cdot (c \mathbf{v}_s)
= \nabla \cdot (D_t \nabla c),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_s$ is the settling velocity and $D_t$ is turbulent eddy diffusivity.

\subsection{Exner Bed Evolution}
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial z_b}{\partial t}
+ \frac{1}{1 - p_0}\,\nabla \cdot \mathbf{q}_b
= \frac{D_b - E_b}{\rho_s (1 - p_0)},
\end{equation}
where $p_0$ is bed porosity, $\mathbf{q}_b$ is bedload flux, and $D_b$, $E_b$ are deposition and erosion fluxes.

\section{Autonomous Validation Protocol}

\subsection{Phase 1: Hydrodynamic Core Initialization \& Invariant Checks}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Mass balance: continuity residuals $<10^{-6}$.
    \item Momentum stability: turbulent stress and drag convergence under quiescent states.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Phase 2: Two-Test Benchmark Suite}

\subsubsection{Test A: Dam-Break Sediment Surge}
A 100\,m channel with erodible bed. The HLLC solver must capture shock front, rarefaction, and bed excavation without oscillations.

\subsubsection{Test B: Beji--Battjes Submerged Bar}
A submerged trapezoidal bar with 1:20 upslope, 2\,m crest, and 1:10 downslope. Validates shoaling, harmonic generation, and energy dissipation against laboratory gauge data.

\subsection{Phase 3: Automated Tolerance Gates}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Free-surface RMSE $<5\%$ relative to gauge data.
    \item Exner stability: non-divergent bed evolution under breaking waves.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Phase 4: Optimization Clearance}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Gatekeeper protocol: no shoreline modification until both benchmarks converge.
    \item Autonomous adaptation: BIA-1 adjusts barrier geometries, porosity, and breakwater alignments after clearance.
\end{itemize}

\section{Multi-Agent Scientific Intelligence Architecture}

\subsection{Telemetry Ingestion Agent}
Ingests meteorological, oceanic, bathymetric, and satellite data into immutable vector states.

\subsection{Physics Agent}
Executes Navier--Stokes, sediment transport, Exner evolution, and boundary friction kernels.

\subsection{Optimization Agent}
Runs gradient-based minimization to tune geometry and material parameters.

\subsection{Anomaly Detection Agent}
Monitors conservation invariants, shear stress thresholds, and numerical singularities.

\subsection{Mission Control Agent}
Orchestrates multi-node execution, enforces tolerance gates, and commits final designs.

\section{Conclusion}

We establish a rigorous autonomous coastal stabilization framework integrating multi-phase hydrodynamics, sediment transport, and multi-agent scientific intelligence. The two-test validation suite ensures solver fidelity across both extreme shock and dispersive wave regimes, enabling trustworthy autonomous shoreline adaptation.

